      "title": "Reaction Time Scores – What Do They Mean?",
      "meta": "Understand your reaction time scores. Learn what 170ms means, how device latency affects results, and why practice matters. Not a medical test.",
      "h1": "Reaction Time Scores: A Practical Guide",
      "intro": "Reaction time tests measure how quickly you respond to a stimulus. Scores vary by device, practice, and individual factors. This guide helps you interpret your results without overclaiming.",
      "quick_answer": "Average reaction time is around 200-250ms. Scores below 200ms are considered fast, but device latency can affect results.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "What Is a Good Reaction Time?",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Typical reaction times range from 200ms to 250ms for simple visual stimuli. Scores below 200ms are often seen in gamers or athletes, but device latency (monitor, mouse, browser) can add 10-50ms.",
            "A score of 170ms is very fast, but it may be influenced by your setup. For a more reliable measure, take the test multiple times and use the median."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Device Latency and Practice Effects",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Your monitor's refresh rate, mouse response, and browser processing all add small delays. These can make your score appear slower or faster than your true reaction time.",
            "Practice also improves scores. The first few attempts may be slower as you learn the test. Take at least 5-10 trials for a stable result."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "What Reaction Time Does NOT Measure",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Reaction time tests are not diagnostic. They don't measure intelligence, health, or cognitive ability. They simply reflect how quickly you respond to a visual cue.",
            "If you have concerns about your reaction time, consult a medical professional – not an online test."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "faq": [
        {
          "q": "Is 170ms reaction time good?",
          "a": "170ms is faster than average, but device latency can affect it. For a reliable measure, test multiple times and use the median."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can I improve my reaction time?",
          "a": "Practice can help, but gains are usually small. Focus on consistency and reducing distractions during the test."
        },
        {
          "q": "Does reaction time indicate intelligence?",
          "a": "No. Reaction time is a simple motor response and does not measure intelligence or cognitive ability."
        }
      ],
