          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Score Interpretation Without IQ Claims",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Typical scores range from 5 to 10 digits. Scores above 9 are rare and often require strategy or practice. Some people use mnemonic techniques to improve.",
            "Avoid comparing your score to IQ or intelligence. The test is a single measure of one type of memory."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Memory Strategies and Device/Practice Caveats",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Strategies like chunking (grouping digits) or visualizing a path can help. Practice also improves scores – the first few attempts may be lower.",
            "Device latency (monitor, mouse) can affect timing. Use a consistent setup for reliable comparisons."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "faq": [
        {
          "q": "What is a good chimp test score?",
          "a": "Scores of 7-9 are typical. Scores above 9 are uncommon and may reflect practice or strategy."
        },
        {
          "q": "Does the chimp test measure intelligence?",
          "a": "No. It measures short-term visual memory for numbers, not general intelligence or cognitive ability."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can I improve my score?",
          "a": "Yes. Practice and memory strategies like chunking can help. But the test is a specific skill, not a measure of overall ability."
        }
      ],
